WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
- Good morning, Believe Nation!

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:05.219
Today's message is Create What You Like.

00:00:05.219 --> 00:00:07.271
Over to you, Seth Rogan.

00:00:07.271 --> 00:00:09.043
(rooster crows)

00:00:09.043 --> 00:00:09.876
("Entspresso" theme song)

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:12.167
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:14.857
♫ Entspresso keep me goin'

00:00:14.857 --> 00:00:19.108
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:19.108 --> 00:00:20.914
- We make the movies we want to go see.

00:00:20.914 --> 00:00:23.971
We found that when you go in meetings,

00:00:23.971 --> 00:00:25.510
there's a lot of, you know, with studios

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.463
and various producers and stuff,

00:00:27.463 --> 00:00:29.989
there's a lot of talk about
who will like the movies.

00:00:29.989 --> 00:00:32.127
You know, you make this movie,
these people will like it.

00:00:32.127 --> 00:00:33.987
You make this movie, and
these people will like it.

00:00:33.987 --> 00:00:37.153
And they're all kind of guesses,
stabs in the dark almost.

00:00:37.153 --> 00:00:38.674
And then, that's when
we realize, you know,

00:00:38.674 --> 00:00:41.292
the only people that we know
that we can make movies for

00:00:41.292 --> 00:00:42.372
is ourselves.

00:00:42.372 --> 00:00:44.424
So, when we sit down to write a movie,

00:00:44.424 --> 00:00:45.477
like "Pineapple Express,"

00:00:45.477 --> 00:00:48.429
we thought, what movie
do we want to go see?

00:00:48.429 --> 00:00:50.777
What's the trailer that
we watch, that we say,

00:00:50.777 --> 00:00:53.396
"that's the movie that I'm going
to throw down my money for.

00:00:53.396 --> 00:00:55.500
That's the movie I
can't wait to go watch."

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.087
And, yeah, that's really all it is.

00:00:58.087 --> 00:00:59.678
You know, you could guess all day,

00:00:59.678 --> 00:01:01.512
as to what other people would like.

00:01:01.512 --> 00:01:03.086
But you know what you like.

00:01:03.086 --> 00:01:04.266
So, that's what we try to do,

00:01:04.266 --> 00:01:06.271
is just stuff that we
would want to go see.

00:01:06.271 --> 00:01:07.112
(whoosh)

00:01:07.112 --> 00:01:07.945
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:01:07.945 --> 00:01:09.755
The question of the day today is,

00:01:09.755 --> 00:01:12.768
I'm curious, what is the
last thing that you created

00:01:12.768 --> 00:01:15.999
that you really really
really liked yourself?

00:01:15.999 --> 00:01:17.537
You created for yourself?

00:01:17.537 --> 00:01:19.210
What was the last thing that you made?

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:20.281
Leave it in the comments below,

00:01:20.281 --> 00:01:22.012
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:01:22.012 --> 00:01:24.096
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to Noel Brosnan.

00:01:24.096 --> 00:01:26.581
Noel, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:01:26.581 --> 00:01:29.989
"Your One Word," it really
really really means a lot to me.

00:01:29.989 --> 00:01:31.222
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:01:31.222 --> 00:01:32.620
I believe in you.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.893
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:01:34.893 --> 00:01:37.179
and whatever your one word is.

00:01:37.179 --> 00:01:39.564
Much love, and I'll see
you again tomorrow morning,

00:01:39.564 --> 00:01:42.096
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:01:42.096 --> 00:01:44.642
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:01:44.642 --> 00:01:48.322
♫ Entspresso keep me goin'

00:01:48.322 --> 00:01:49.155
Hey Believe Nation!

00:01:49.155 --> 00:01:50.788
If you want to stay inspired

00:01:50.788 --> 00:01:52.883
on creating the things that you like,

00:01:52.883 --> 00:01:55.640
stay tuned for some special bonus clips.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.473
- I don't think in terms of, like,

00:01:58.473 --> 00:02:00.541
restrospectively or retroactively

00:02:00.541 --> 00:02:02.827
looking at what I did and
how I got to where I am.

00:02:02.827 --> 00:02:04.970
Instead, it's much more focused on,

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:06.083
what do I want to do next?

00:02:06.083 --> 00:02:06.916
- Mmhmm.

00:02:06.916 --> 00:02:08.447
- Because I think that
if you try to consider,

00:02:08.447 --> 00:02:10.716
or cater to your audience too much,

00:02:10.716 --> 00:02:14.466
you'll invariably end up
making something that's mush.

00:02:14.466 --> 00:02:15.299
- Yeah.

00:02:15.299 --> 00:02:16.638
- Because I don't know
that the audience knows

00:02:16.638 --> 00:02:17.888
what they want to see.

00:02:17.888 --> 00:02:20.387
I know that I don't know what's
going to pique my interest.

00:02:20.387 --> 00:02:22.735
If I knew how to describe
the perfect movie,

00:02:22.735 --> 00:02:25.274
blog, television show, I would just do it.

00:02:25.274 --> 00:02:27.779
I want to see something that surprises me.

00:02:27.779 --> 00:02:30.176
And, when I think of my own
work, that's exactly right.

00:02:30.176 --> 00:02:32.032
Like, I put my head down,

00:02:32.032 --> 00:02:33.715
and I try to ignore the world,

00:02:33.715 --> 00:02:36.081
and I try to make something
that I think is really good.

00:02:36.081 --> 00:02:36.914
- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:36.914 --> 00:02:37.879
- And if people like, it great.

00:02:37.879 --> 00:02:40.920
If they don't, I don't
care, as long as I like it.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.964
So I think that if I had
one thing to attribute

00:02:43.964 --> 00:02:45.727
all of this growth to,

00:02:45.727 --> 00:02:47.697
it's making work that I feel embodies

00:02:47.697 --> 00:02:50.732
the same level of
integrity of anything else

00:02:50.732 --> 00:02:52.394
that I would invest myself into.

00:02:52.394 --> 00:02:55.958
- Do you have a specific target audience?

00:02:55.958 --> 00:02:58.837
- Well just anybody who can
read, anybody who's literate.

00:02:58.837 --> 00:03:03.679
It used to be, in Canada, comics
were just for little kids.

00:03:03.679 --> 00:03:06.370
Or they were thought
to be for little kids.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:07.203
- [Interviewer] But that's changed.

00:03:07.203 --> 00:03:10.607
- Yeah, with Marvel, 'cause
we started directing Marvel

00:03:10.607 --> 00:03:12.064
at ourselves, really.

00:03:12.064 --> 00:03:14.494
The theory was, we're going
to write the kind of stories

00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:16.916
we would enjoy reading.

00:03:16.916 --> 00:03:17.752
You see?

00:03:17.752 --> 00:03:20.651
And I didn't know if it would work or not.

00:03:20.651 --> 00:03:23.601
But, it worked better
than anybody expected,

00:03:23.601 --> 00:03:27.280
because, we grabbed a lot of
readers who are much older.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.583
Who liked the kind of
things that we liked.

00:03:29.583 --> 00:03:31.977
But we didn't lose the young kids.

00:03:31.977 --> 00:03:35.765
So we ended up, we had kids
seven years old who read Marvel,

00:03:35.765 --> 00:03:39.069
who don't understand
the subtle implications

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:42.613
and ramifications and the
psychology and philosophy

00:03:42.613 --> 00:03:44.728
and the subliminal moralizing,

00:03:44.728 --> 00:03:47.375
but they loved the nutty
characters and the long underwear

00:03:47.375 --> 00:03:49.254
and the colorful costumes.

00:03:49.254 --> 00:03:53.421
And, the older readers enjoyed
all the little subplots,

00:03:54.329 --> 00:03:56.109
and the little asides we'd put in,

00:03:56.109 --> 00:03:59.135
and the little things that a
young kid can't ever grasp.

00:03:59.135 --> 00:04:01.483
But, they're there in the story.

00:04:01.483 --> 00:04:02.489
- You've reached a happy medium.

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:06.171
- Yeah, so we find the best
formula, if you call it that,

00:04:06.171 --> 00:04:08.447
is we don't try to write for any audience,

00:04:08.447 --> 00:04:11.146
we try to write stories we would enjoy,

00:04:11.146 --> 00:04:13.459
and apparently there are
enough people in the world

00:04:13.459 --> 00:04:14.693
with the same tastes.

00:04:14.693 --> 00:04:15.887
I hate to say that,

00:04:15.887 --> 00:04:17.923
I'd like to think my taste is far better,

00:04:17.923 --> 00:04:19.308
but apparently everybody

00:04:19.308 --> 00:04:21.054
has got pretty much the same taste,

00:04:21.054 --> 00:04:22.421
'cause the books sell pretty well.

00:04:22.421 --> 00:04:24.085
- When it comes to pleasing people,

00:04:24.085 --> 00:04:27.172
listen, of course you want
to have a good reception,

00:04:27.172 --> 00:04:29.486
but, you don't make a
movie without committing.

00:04:29.486 --> 00:04:32.336
And, so I'd have to make a
movie that I would want to see,

00:04:32.336 --> 00:04:34.894
and I have to create a character
that I am interested in,

00:04:34.894 --> 00:04:35.891
and that's it.

00:04:35.891 --> 00:04:38.312
I put my head down, and I focus on that,

00:04:38.312 --> 00:04:40.427
and I don't really pay attention

00:04:40.427 --> 00:04:42.271
to what people may be requesting,

00:04:42.271 --> 00:04:44.612
because I believe that if
we have enough conviction

00:04:44.612 --> 00:04:46.932
in what we're doing, people
will believe it as well.

00:04:46.932 --> 00:04:48.911
- Do you think the secret to your success,

00:04:48.911 --> 00:04:50.830
is actually, you are, in many ways,

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.187
in the ways you lead your life,

00:04:52.187 --> 00:04:54.779
your tastes are quite
average, middle-of-the-road,

00:04:54.779 --> 00:04:57.336
the kind of stuff that the
mass market would like?

00:04:57.336 --> 00:04:58.984
- For sure, absolutely right.

00:04:58.984 --> 00:05:02.625
And I don't think you can make records,

00:05:02.625 --> 00:05:05.073
I don't think you can
make TV shows properly,

00:05:05.073 --> 00:05:09.022
unless you can sit there
as a listener or a viewer.

00:05:09.022 --> 00:05:10.830
You can't guess what people like.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.351
It would be like me guessing
what you want for dinner.

00:05:13.351 --> 00:05:14.184
It's impossible.

00:05:14.184 --> 00:05:16.144
I can tell you what I want.

00:05:16.144 --> 00:05:19.215
So I base everything on my own tastes.

00:05:19.215 --> 00:05:21.415
And I have, you know, fairly broad,

00:05:21.415 --> 00:05:23.331
I'd say quite simple tastes.

00:05:23.331 --> 00:05:26.024
- I think originality, period,

00:05:26.024 --> 00:05:28.341
could crack not only the UK, anywhere.

00:05:28.341 --> 00:05:32.050
Originality, people are
straying away from that.

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:34.099
Every time people do music or anything,

00:05:34.099 --> 00:05:37.789
they do it like someone
who has done it before,

00:05:37.789 --> 00:05:40.158
or they do it like what
think the people want.

00:05:40.158 --> 00:05:41.075
I don't do that.

00:05:41.075 --> 00:05:44.161
I do what I know is nice to me.

00:05:44.161 --> 00:05:46.032
I do what I think is cool.

00:05:46.032 --> 00:05:47.705
And if you don't think it's cool,

00:05:47.705 --> 00:05:49.614
then I think you're not cool.

00:05:49.614 --> 00:05:51.927
So, if you approach me with that attitude,

00:05:51.927 --> 00:05:53.729
then I don't think you could ever lose.

